    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d19">

                <pb facs="0036" n="20" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg20"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710 Consultation 4/7–1247: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in Paraguay </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-07-15T20:00:00-04:00">July 15, 1947—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>190. Urtel 377, July 12.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d19-f1"/> US draft treaty does not
                    attempt to define “aggression” since determination whether aggression is being
                    prepared or threat of aggression exists would be made in each case through
                    consultation of all parties on initiative any one of them. Aggression can take
                    so many forms in modern world that definition not practicable <pb facs="0037"
                        n="21" xml:id="pg21"/> and any attempt to define would limit free
                    determination of parties in above consultations. Language of US draft would
                    allow subversion or political attack aimed at subversion by any state against
                    any American state to be regarded as threat of aggression if consultation so
                    determines. Enforcement measures to meet threat of aggression could be taken
                    only with authority Security Council in accordance Art. 53 UN Charter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
